Description: external security rating with evidence and remediation steps
 OpenScore looks at an organization from the outside, the way an attacker
 would, and grades it A-F across eight factors: email security, DNS health,
 TLS/certificates, application security, network exposure, patching cadence,
 IP reputation and information leakage.
 .
 Every finding comes with the evidence that triggered it, a command to verify
 it by hand, and remediation steps. The result is a single self-contained
 HTML report (plus JSON) with PNG evidence exhibits for tickets, a baseline
 diff against the previous scan, and a suppressions file for triaged false
 positives, accepted risks and compensating controls.
 .
 It serves defenders, vulnerability management and the external
 attack-surface review of authorized penetration tests. It identifies and
 documents issues; it never exploits them (no exploitation, credential
 guessing or payload injection).
 .
 Passive and light checks only use public data and normal browser-like
 requests. Active checks (port scan, path probes, CORS, nuclei with safe
 templates, testssl.sh) require --active plus a written authorization
 reference and a scope confirmation, stay in scope, and are audit-logged.
 .
 nmap, nuclei, subfinder and testssl.sh are used when installed;
 python3-playwright adds page screenshots to the evidence.
